entry.summary:
I recently read the discrete flow matching (Gat et al., 2024) paper and found it very interesting. However, it is technically dense and frequently draws comparisons to continuous flow matching, a topic I’m not familiar with. Below are my reading notes of Gat et al. (2024)(Gat et al., 2024). I’ve expanded on the derivations and technical details to make the concept of discrete flow matching self-contained, without requiring prior knowledge of the continuous framework. Probability path on discrete spaces Let $\mathcal{V} \coloneqq {1, \ldots V}$ be the vocabulary, and let $\mathcal{S} \coloneqq \mathcal{V}^L$ be the set of all possible sequences of length $L$. Let ${Y_t : t \in [0, 1]}$ be a continuous-time stochastic process, where each $Y_t$ takes values in the sequence space $S$. We denote the probability mass function (PMF) of the random variable $Y_t$ by $p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) \coloneqq \mathbb{P}(Y_t =  oldsymbol{y})$. While the subscript notation $p_{Y_t}$ is heavier than the typical shorthand $p_t$, explicitly tracking random variables is useful, as we will see in coming sections. We assume that $(Y_t)_t$ is a continuous-time Markov chain: for all states $ oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z} \in S$, all times $0 \le u &lt; s &lt; t$, and any past state $ oldsymbol{w}$: \[p_{Y_t|Y_s, Y_u}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{w}) = p_{Y_t|Y_s}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}).\] In generative modeling, we parametrize a probability path $p_{Y_t}: S 	o [0,1]$ connecting a simple initial noise distribution $p_{Y_0}$ to a data distribution $p_{Y_1}$. We achieve this by modeling $ rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}$, the time evolution of the PMF. Probability velocity To study $ rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}$, it is convenient to gain intuition with matrix notation. Let $ oldsymbol{p}_{Y_t} \coloneqq [p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y})]_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 	imes \lvert S vert}$ be the row vector of marginal probabilities. One way to describe how $ oldsymbol{p}_{Y_t}$ evolves over time is through a time-dependent matrix $ oldsymbol{Q}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{\lvert S vert 	imes \lvert S vert}$ such that \[ rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t}  oldsymbol{p}_{Y_t} =  oldsymbol{p}_{Y_t}  oldsymbol{Q}_t.\] The equation above motivates the definition of velocity function, defined below. The velocity is a function $q_t: \mathcal{S} 	imes \mathcal{S} 	o \mathbb{R}$ as the entry-wise specification of $ oldsymbol{Q}_t = [q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z})]_{ oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}}$. Definition 1 (Velocity). A time-dependent function $q_t: \mathcal{S} 	imes \mathcal{S} 	o \mathbb{R}$ is a velocity function if for all $t$: $q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) \ge 0$ for all $ oldsymbol{y} 
eq  oldsymbol{z}$. $\sum_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S} q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) = 0$ for all $ oldsymbol{y}$. Definition 1 extends the standard definition for time-homogeneous Markov chains (Norris, 1998), where $q = q_t$ is time-independent. The definition implies that the diagonal entries represent the total outflow from a state: \[q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{y}) = - \sum_{ oldsymbol{z} 
eq  oldsymbol{y}} q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}).\] A velocity function preserves probability mass over time, as shown in the proposition below. Proposition 2 (Velocity preserves probability distributions). Let $q_t$ be a velocity function and $p_0$ be a valid PMF. Let $p_t$ evolve according to: $$  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_t( oldsymbol{z}) \coloneqq \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_t( oldsymbol{y}) q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}). $$ Then $p_t$ remains a valid PMF for all $t \ge 0$. Proof. Summing the equation over $ oldsymbol{z}$ yields $$  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} \sum_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S} p_t( oldsymbol{z}) = \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_t( oldsymbol{y}) \underbrace{\sum_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S} q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z})}_{=0} = 0. $$ Since $\sum_{ oldsymbol{z}} p_0( oldsymbol{z}) = 1$, the sum is conserved. We now show non-negativity. Since $p_t$ satisfies the differential equation, it is differentiable (hence continuous) in $t$. Suppose $p_t$ is not always non-negative for a sequence $ oldsymbol{z} \in S$. Since $p_0( oldsymbol{z}) \ge 0$ and $p_t( oldsymbol{z})$ is continuous, by the intermediate value theorem there exists a first time $s$ such that $p_s( oldsymbol{z}) = 0$ and $\left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_t( oldsymbol{z}) ight|_{t=s} &lt; 0$ for some $ oldsymbol{z}$. At this instant, $$ \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_t( oldsymbol{z}) ight|_{t=s} = \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} 
eq  oldsymbol{z}} p_s( oldsymbol{y}) q_s( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}). $$ Since $s$ is the first exit time, $p_s( oldsymbol{y}) \ge 0$. By definition, $q_s( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) \ge 0$ for $ oldsymbol{y} 
eq  oldsymbol{z}$. Thus the derivative must be non-negative, a contradiction. &#9633; Proposition 2 shows that any velocity function preserves probability mass over time. However, for a given path ${p_{Y_t}}_t$, it is unclear if the PMFs defined through the master equation are compatible with the given $p_{Y_t}$ at all time $t$. Definition 3 defines such consistency. Definition 3 (Generation). A continuous function $q_t: \mathcal{S} 	imes \mathcal{S} 	o \mathbb{R}$ generates the probability path $p_{Y_t}$ if, for all $ oldsymbol{z} \in S$ and $t \in [0, 1]$, $$  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z}) = \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) q_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}). $$ Note that multiple velocity functions can generate the same marginal path. Indeed, the linear system is underdetermined for large state spaces ($\lvert S vert$ constraints for $\lvert S vert^2$ entries). Below, we see one example of the velocity function that generates $p_{Y_t}$ and is explicitly defined through $p_{Y_t}$. Proposition 4. Let $Y_t$ be a continuous-time Markov Chain. The instantaneous conditional derivative function $u_t: \mathcal{S} 	imes \mathcal{S} 	o \mathbb{R}$, defined as $$u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) \coloneqq \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s} p_{Y_s|Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}) ight\vert_{s=t},$$ is a velocity function and it generates $p_{Y_t}$. Proof. For $ oldsymbol{y} 
eq  oldsymbol{z}$, $u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) = \lim_{h 	o 0^+} h^{-1} [p_{Y_{t+h} \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - 0] \ge 0$. Summing over $ oldsymbol{z}$, $$ \sum_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S} u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) = \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s} \sum_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S} p_{Y_s|Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}) ight\vert_{s=t} = \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s} (1) ight\vert_{s=t} = 0. $$ Thus $u_t$ is a velocity function. To show generation, we expand the time derivative: $$  egin{aligned}  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z}) &amp;= \lim_{h 	o 0^+}  rac{1}{h} \left[ \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) p_{Y_{t+h} \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) \mathbb{I}( oldsymbol{y}= oldsymbol{z}) ight] \ &amp;= \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) \left[ \lim_{h 	o 0^+}  rac{p_{Y_{t+h}|Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - p_{Y_t|Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y})}{h} ight] \ &amp;= \sum_{ oldsymbol{y} \in S} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}). \end{aligned} $$ &#9633; Factorized, per-token velocity As we will see in a later section, at each time $t$, we would like to parametrize the mapping $ oldsymbol{y} \mapsto [u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z})]_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S}$ through a neural network. However, without imposing any constraints on $u_t$, this parametrization is computationally intractable. Indeed, for a given $ oldsymbol{y} \in S$, the matrix $[u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z})]_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S}$ contains $V^L$ entries, scaling exponentially with the sequence length $L$. To make the modeling of $u_t$ computationally feasible, we assume transitions occur at most one token at a time. We decompose $u_t$ into local velocity functions $u_t^\ell$, where $\ell$ is a token index in a sequence: \[u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) =  egin{cases} u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z^\ell) &amp; 	ext{if }  oldsymbol{y} 	ext{ and }  oldsymbol{z} 	ext{ differ only at position } \ell \ \sum_{\ell=1}^L u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, y^\ell) &amp; 	ext{if }  oldsymbol{y} =  oldsymbol{z} \ 0 &amp; 	ext{if }  oldsymbol{y} 	ext{ and }  oldsymbol{z} 	ext{ differ at more than one position}. \end{cases}\] Note that this implies that \(u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, y^\ell) \coloneqq - \sum_{w 
eq y^\ell} u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, w),\) which follows from the fact that $0 = \sum_{ oldsymbol{z} \in S} u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}) = u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, y^\ell) + \sum_{w 
eq y^\ell} u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, w).$ The advantage of the factoring a global $u_t$ to a local velocity $u_t^\ell$ is that it reduces the complexity from exponential to linear in $L$. For each $ oldsymbol{y}$, the mapping $ oldsymbol{y} \mapsto \Big [[u_t^1( oldsymbol{y}, z)]_{z \in \mathcal{V}}, \ldots, [u_t^L( oldsymbol{y}, z)]_{z \in \mathcal{V}} \Big],$ has only $V \cdot L$ values. But what is the interpretation of each local velocity $u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z)$? In the following, we show that the local velocity $u_t^\ell$ is the per-token instantaneous change of probability. Proposition 5. The local velocity $u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z)$ is the instantaneous rate at which the $\ell$-th token of $ oldsymbol{y}$ transitions from its current value to $z$: $$u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z) = \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s} ight\vert_{s=t} p_{Y_s^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}).$$ Proof. By the definition of the marginal PMF, $$ p_{Y_s^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) = \sum_{ oldsymbol{w} \in S} \underbrace{p_{Y_s|Y_t}( oldsymbol{w} \mid  oldsymbol{y})}_{	ext{seq. transition}} \cdot \underbrace{\mathbb{I}(z= oldsymbol{w}^\ell)}_{	ext{token check}}.$$ Using the global velocity function $u_t$, $$ \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s} p_{Y_s^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) ight\vert_{s=t} = \sum_{ oldsymbol{w} \in S} u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{w}) \mathbb{I}(z= oldsymbol{w}^\ell). $$ Note that on the RHS, the term $u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{w})$ vanishes unless $ oldsymbol{w}$ differs from $ oldsymbol{y}$ in at most one position. If $z 
eq y^\ell$, the indicator $\mathbb{I}(z= oldsymbol{w}^\ell)$ requires $ oldsymbol{w}$ to differ from $ oldsymbol{y}$ at index $\ell$. So $u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{w})$ is nonzero only if $ oldsymbol{w}$ matches $ oldsymbol{y}$ at all indices except the $\ell$-th index. The sum thus collapses to the single term $u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z)$. If $z = y^\ell$, the RHS becomes: $$  egin{aligned} \sum_{ oldsymbol{w} \in S} u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{w}) \mathbb{I}(y^\ell=w^\ell) &amp;= u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{y}) + \sum_{j 
eq \ell} \sum_{w 
eq y^j} u_t^j( oldsymbol{y}, w) \ &amp; = -\sum_{j=1}^L \sum_{w 
eq y^j} u_t^j( oldsymbol{y}, w) + \sum_{j 
eq \ell} \sum_{w 
eq y^j} u_t^j( oldsymbol{y}, w) \ &amp; = - \sum_{w 
eq y^\ell} u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, w), \end{aligned} $$ which is $u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, y^\ell)$ by construction. &#9633; Discrete flow matching The key intuition behind discrete flow matching (Gat et al., 2024) is that, to model $p_{Y_t}$, we use as much information as possible from $p_0$ and $p_1$, which we can draw samples from. Let $\pi( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)$ be a joint distribution over noise sequences and data sequences; we typically assume the noise and the data distribution are independent: $\pi( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = p_{Y_0}( oldsymbol{y}_0) p_{Y_1}( oldsymbol{y}_1)$. We define the marginal probability path $p_{Y_t}$ by marginalizing over these pairs: \(p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z}) = \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \in S} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) \pi( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1),\) where $p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)$ is a conditional probability path. Furthermore, we impose two assumptions: Boundary constraints: $p_{Y_0}(\cdot \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = \mathbb{I}( oldsymbol{y}_0 = \cdot)$ and $p_{Y_1}(\cdot \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = \mathbb{I}( oldsymbol{y}_1=\cdot)$. Token independence conditioned on endpoints: \(p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = \prod_{\ell=1}^L p_{Y_t}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1).\) A simple way to satisfy these constraints is to parametrize the path $p_{Y_t}$ based on convex combination of a noise sequence and a sampled sequence: \[ orall \ell \in [L]: \quad p_{Y_t}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = (1 - \sigma_t) \mathbb{I}(y_0^\ell=z^\ell) + \sigma_t \mathbb{I}(y_1^\ell=z^\ell),\] In the following, we first find a velocity function that generates the conditional distribution, and then find a velocity function that generates the marginal distribution. Generating the conditional probability path We verify that the velocity function derived in the standard form generates the convex conditional probability path. Proposition 6. Let $\sigma_t: [0, 1] 	o [0, 1]$ be a differentiable, strictly increasing function with $\sigma_0=0, \sigma_1=1$. The conditional probability path defined by $$ p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = (1 - \sigma_t) \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_0^\ell) + \sigma_t \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) $$ is generated by the velocity function: $$ u_t^\ell(y^\ell, z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) =  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \left[ \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell) ight]. $$ Proof. Let $\gamma_t \coloneqq  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t}$. Since $\dot{\sigma}_t \ge 0$ and $\sigma_t &lt; 1$ for $t &lt; 1$, we have $\gamma_t \ge 0$. 1. Validity. For $z^\ell 
eq y^\ell$, $u_t^\ell(y^\ell, z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = \gamma_t \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) \ge 0$. Summing over $z^\ell$: $$ \sum_{z^\ell} u_t^\ell(y^\ell, z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = \gamma_t \left[ \sum_{z^\ell} \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \sum_{z^\ell} \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell) ight] = \gamma_t(1 - 1) = 0. $$ 2. Generation. We verify the definition of generation. The LHS is the time derivative: $$  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) = \dot{\sigma}_t \left[ \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_0^\ell) ight]. $$ The RHS is the expected velocity: $$  egin{aligned} &amp;\sum_{y^\ell} p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(y^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) u_t^\ell(y^\ell, z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) \ &amp;= \sum_{y^\ell} p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(y^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) \gamma_t \left[ \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell) ight] \ &amp;= \gamma_t \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) \underbrace{\sum_{y^\ell} p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(y^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)}_{1} - \gamma_t \underbrace{\sum_{y^\ell} p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(y^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell)}_{p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)} \ &amp;= \gamma_t \left[ \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - p_{Y_t^\ell \mid Y_0, Y_1}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) ight]. \end{aligned} $$ Substituting the path definition into the bracket: $$  egin{aligned} 	ext{RHS} &amp;= \gamma_t \left( \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \left[ (1 - \sigma_t) \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_0^\ell) + \sigma_t \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) ight] ight) \ &amp;= \gamma_t (1 - \sigma_t) \left[ \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_0^\ell) ight]. \end{aligned} $$ Since $\gamma_t (1 - \sigma_t) = \dot{\sigma}_t$, the RHS equals the LHS. &#9633; Generating the marginal probability path We now relate the conditional velocity functions derived above to the marginal velocity function required to generate the marginal path $p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z})$. Proposition 7 (Marginal Velocity Construction). Let $u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)$ be a conditional local velocity function generating the conditional probability path. The marginal local velocity function $u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z^\ell)$, defined by the posterior expectation: $$ u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z^\ell) \coloneqq \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1} u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) p_{Y_0, Y_1 \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \mid  oldsymbol{y}), $$ generates the marginal probability path $p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z})$. Proof. We compute the time derivative of the marginal path directly. By linearity of the derivative: $$  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z}) = \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1} \left[  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t \mid Y_0, Y_1}( oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) ight] \pi( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1). $$ Let $u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)$ be the global conditional velocity. Since it generates the conditional path: $$  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z}) = \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1} \left[ \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}} p_{Y_t \mid Y_0, Y_1}( oldsymbol{y} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) ight] \pi( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1). $$ Using Bayes' rule, the term $p_{Y_t \mid Y_0, Y_1}( oldsymbol{y} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) \pi( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1)$ can be factored as $p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) p_{Y_0, Y_1 \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \mid  oldsymbol{y})$. $$  egin{aligned}  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}t} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{z}) &amp;= \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) \left[ \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1} u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z} \mid  oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1) p_{Y_0, Y_1 \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \mid  oldsymbol{y}) ight] \ &amp;= \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}} p_{Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}) u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z}). \end{aligned} $$ Thus, the marginal global velocity $u_t( oldsymbol{y},  oldsymbol{z})$ is the expectation of the conditional global velocity. By linearity, this relationship holds element-wise for the local velocity functions. &#9633; Applying Proposition 7 to the conditional velocity function derived earlier, we obtain the explicit form of the marginal velocity function for the convex path: \[ egin{aligned} u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z^\ell) &amp;= \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1}  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \left[ \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell) ight] p_{Y_0, Y_1 \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \mid  oldsymbol{y}) \ &amp;=  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \left[ \sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1} \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y_1^\ell) p_{Y_0, Y_1 \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell) \underbrace{\sum_{ oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1} p_{Y_0, Y_1 \mid Y_t}( oldsymbol{y}_0,  oldsymbol{y}_1 \mid  oldsymbol{y})}_{1} ight] \ &amp;=  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \left[ p_{Y_1^\ell \mid Y_t}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - \mathbb{I}(z^\ell = y^\ell) ight]. \end{aligned}\] Here, the term $p_{Y_1^\ell \mid Y_t}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y})$ corresponds to the probability denoiser. It is the probability that the true data token at position $\ell$ is $z^\ell$, given the current noisy sequence $ oldsymbol{y}$. Machine Learning Takeaway The final equation above shows that the marginal velocity is entirely determined by the conditional probability of the clean data given the noisy state, $p_{Y_1^\ell \mid Y_t}(z^\ell \mid  oldsymbol{y})$. This allows a simple recipe for training generative models through learning a denoising neural network. Add noise to sequence Let $M_t$ be a Bernoulli random variable that indicates noise, with $\mathbb{P}(M_t^l=1)=\sigma_t$ for all $l \in {0, \ldots, L}$. We let $Y_t$ be the random variable taking values in partially corrupted sequences: \(Y_t^l = (1 - M_t^l) \circ Y_0 + M_t^l \circ Y_1,\) for all $l \in {0, \ldots, L}$. Parametrization We introduce a transformer $p_	heta$ defined as: \[ egin{aligned} p_	heta \colon &amp; \mathcal{S} 	imes [0,1] &amp;&amp; 	o \mathbb{R}^{V 	imes L} \ &amp; ( oldsymbol{y}, t) &amp;&amp; \mapsto p_{	heta}( oldsymbol{y}, t) \end{aligned}\] which takes a noisy sequence $ oldsymbol{y}$ and a time $t$ as input, and outputs a categorical distribution over the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$ for each position. The training objective of the denoising network is to minimize the negative log-likelihood of the clean token $Y_1^\ell \in \mathcal{V}$ given the noisy input $ oldsymbol{y}_t \in S$ at the time $t$: \[\mathcal{L}(	heta) = \mathbb{E}_{t, Y_0, Y_1, Y_t} \left[ - \sum_{\ell=1}^L \log \Big(p_	heta(Y_t, t)\Big)[\ell, Y_1^\ell] ight].\] Once trained, we can compute the estimated velocity field $\hat{u}_t^\ell$ required for generation by simply plugging the network output into the velocity equation: \[\hat{u}_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z) =  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \left[ p_	heta( oldsymbol{y}, t)[\ell, {z}] - \mathbb{I}(z = y^\ell) ight].\] Training The training process utilizes the concept of simulation-free training (or flow matching). Instead of simulating a trajectory to find the loss, we sample the probability path directly. Sample Data: Draw a clean sequence $ oldsymbol{y}_1 \sim p_{	ext{data}}$ and a noise sequence $ oldsymbol{y}_0 \sim p_{	ext{noise}}$ (e.g., uniform random tokens). Sample Time: Draw a time step $t \sim 	ext{Uniform}([0, 1])$. Corrupt (Interpolate): Construct a noisy sample $ oldsymbol{y}_t$ using the convex conditional path. For every position $\ell$, we sample $Y_t^\ell$ independently: \[y_t^\ell =  egin{cases} y_1^\ell &amp; 	ext{with probability } \sigma_t \ y_0^\ell &amp; 	ext{with probability } 1 - \sigma_t \end{cases}\] This is equivalent to noise injection where the noise level is determined by $t$. Optimize: Minimize the loss function $\mathcal{L}(	heta)$. By minimizing this loss, $p_	heta$ approximates the posterior mean $p_{Y_1 \mid Y_t}$. This learned denoiser is then used in the Euler Sampling scheme described in the next section to generate new data from pure noise. Sampling Suppose we trained the denoising model $p_{	heta}$, how do we use it for sampling? Recall \[u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z) = \left.  rac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s} p_{Y_s^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) ight\vert_{s=t} = \lim_{h 	o 0^{+}}  rac{p_{Y_{t+h}^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - p_{Y_t^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y})}{h}\] In Euler discretizations, we choose $N_{\mathrm{step}}$ such that $h = 1/N_{\mathrm{step}}$ is small: \[ egin{aligned} p_{Y_{t+h}^\ell|Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) &amp; \approx \mathbb{I}(z=y^\ell) + h u_t^\ell( oldsymbol{y}, z) \ &amp; = \mathbb{I}(z = y^\ell) + h  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \left[ p_{Y_1^\ell \mid Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) - \mathbb{I}(z = y^\ell) ight] \ &amp; = \underbrace{\Big(1 - h  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t}\Big)}_{=1-\alpha_t}\mathbb{I}(z = y^\ell) + \underbrace{\Big(h  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t}\Big)}_{\coloneqq \alpha_t}p_{Y_1^\ell \mid Y_t}(z \mid  oldsymbol{y}) \ &amp; \approx (1-\alpha_t)\mathbb{I}(z = y^\ell) + \alpha_t \, p_{ oldsymbol{	heta}}( oldsymbol{y})[\ell,z] \end{aligned}\] The following is the sampling procedure. Note that $N_{\mathrm{step}}$ is large enough such that the update probability $\alpha_t \in [0, 1]$ for all $t$. Initialize: Sample an initial noise sequence $ oldsymbol{y}_0 \sim p_{Y_0}$ (e.g., uniform random tokens). Define a step size $h = 1/N_{\mathrm{step}}$. Iterate: For each step $i = 0, \dots, N_{\mathrm{step}}-1$: Set time $t = i \cdot h$. Compute the update probability $\alpha_t \coloneqq h \cdot  rac{\dot{\sigma}_t}{1 - \sigma_t} \in \mathbb{R}$. Compute denoiser probabilities: $\widehat{ oldsymbol{p}} = p_	heta( oldsymbol{y}_t, t) \in \mathbb{R}^{V 	imes L}$. For each position $\ell = 1, \dots, L$, sample a Bernoulli variable $b^\ell \sim 	ext{Bern}(\alpha_t)$. Update token $\ell$ based on $b^\ell$: \[y_{t+h}^\ell \leftarrow  egin{cases} z \sim 	extrm{Categorical}\Big(\widehat{ oldsymbol{p}}[:, \ell] \Big) &amp; 	ext{if } b^\ell = 1 	ext{ (update)} \ y_t^\ell &amp; 	ext{if } b^\ell = 0 	ext{ (keep)} \end{cases}\] Set $ oldsymbol{y}_{t+h} \leftarrow (y_{t+h}^1, \dots, y_{t+h}^L)$. Return: The final sequence $ oldsymbol{y}_1$. References Gat, I., Remez, T., Shaul, N., Kreuk, F., Chen, R. T. Q., Synnaeve, G., Adi, Y., &amp; Lipman, Y. (2024). Discrete Flow Matching. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems. ↩ Norris, J. (1998). Markov Chains. Cambridge University Press. ↩
